    The white wolf did not follow them. He sat by the trunk, and the Lord of the Forest kept a hand on the wolf’s nape—heavily, the way one lays a stone on the cabbage in a barrel.
}
{ zastaw_u_leszego:
- zastaw_siekiera:
    Only a rusty sliver of the blade still showed from the coil of roots. No one gave him back his axe.
- zastaw_krzesiwo:
    He did not ask about the flint and steel. The Lord of the Forest did not so much as glance towards the hollow.
- zastaw_cien:
    He walked over the lichen, and nothing walked behind him.
}
At the edge of the clearing Jaromir turned round once more. The Lord of the Forest stood beneath the oak, his head among the treetops, and only his eyes glowed in the bark.
“On All Souls’ Day,” he said, and his voice reached Jaromir as clearly as if he were whispering in his ear. “On All Souls’ Day I shall come to the village. Then we shall settle our reckoning, to the very end.”
The branches no longer drew aside for Jaromir. They lashed his face, and he shielded the little one with his body. # dalej
